{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Why Fusion 360 + Structural Member Is Required Today</h2>
          <p>
            RotaryCAM includes an embedded neural network (<code class="mono">FOVNet</code>) that inspects 3D B-Rep solids on STEP import and automatically classifies each tube&rsquo;s cross-section family (<code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_Angle</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_Channel</code>) along with its outer dimensions and wall thickness.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-callout warning" style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            <span class="doc-callout-icon">⚠️</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Current Requirement — Autodesk Fusion 360 + Structural Member Plugin:</strong>
              Because B-Rep STEP files encode surface parameterization and seam topology differently across CAD kernels, the AI profile classifier must be trained on application-specific STEP shapes. Today, the neural network is trained specifically on <strong>Autodesk Fusion 360</strong> assemblies built with the <strong>Structural Member (<code class="mono">SM</code>)</strong> add-in (as currently only Fusion 360 is available in our training pipeline). Follow the 8 steps below to guarantee 100% automatic profile detection.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">CAD Environment</span>
              <span class="value">Autodesk Fusion 360 + <code class="mono">SM</code> Add-In</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Bend Unfolding Prep</span>
              <span class="value"><code class="mono">Modify → Combine</code> Contiguous Tube Bodies</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">CAM Ingestion</span>
              <span class="value">Zero-Click <code class="mono">AI</code> Profile &amp; Pack Grouping</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Open Autodesk Fusion 360 &amp; Verify the Structural Member Add-In</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Launch <strong>Autodesk Fusion 360</strong> and create or save a new design document (for example, <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo</code>):
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Verify the Structural Member Toolbar Icons:</strong> In the <strong>DESIGN &rarr; SOLID &rarr; CREATE</strong> ribbon, confirm that the four blue <strong>Structural Member</strong> icons (<span class="ui-chip">SM</span> Structural Member, Miter, Notch, and Trim/Extend) are visible.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Document Units:</strong> In the left <strong>Browser</strong> tree under <strong>Document Settings</strong>, you can work in millimeters (<code class="mono">mm, g</code>) or inches—while selecting standard imperial or metric tube profiles inside the Structural Member library.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1 — Fusion 360 workspace with the Structural Member (SM) add-in icons in the SOLID toolbar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-1-fusion.png" alt="Step 1: Autodesk Fusion 360 workspace showing the Structural Member SM icons in the CREATE toolbar" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Click <code class="mono">Create Sketch</code> &amp; Select a Reference Plane</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Structural Member bodies are swept along 2D or 3D sketch lines and arcs representing the tube centerline:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Click <span class="ui-chip">Create Sketch</span>:</strong> Click the <strong>Create Sketch</strong> icon at the far left of the <strong>SOLID &rarr; CREATE</strong> toolbar.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Choose an Origin Plane:</strong> Click one of the primary origin planes (<code class="mono">XY</code>, <code class="mono">XZ</code>, or <code class="mono">YZ</code>) in the 3D viewport to enter Sketch mode.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2 — Clicking Create Sketch and selecting a reference origin plane</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-2-sketch.png" alt="Step 2: Clicking Create Sketch and selecting the origin plane in Fusion 360" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Draw Your Tube Centerline Sketch &amp; Click <code class="mono">Finish Sketch</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Lay out the centerline wireframe for your frame or bent tube assembly:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Draw Straight Segments &amp; Tangent Bend Arcs:</strong> Use <strong>Line</strong> (<code class="mono">L</code>) for straight members and <strong>Fillet</strong> (<code class="mono">F</code>) or tangent arcs where a tube will be bent around a centerline radius. In this example, the vertical line on the left represents a straight square HSS post, and the D-shaped loop with two tangent corner radii represents a bent tube that meets the post.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">✓ Finish Sketch</span>:</strong> Click the green <strong>Finish Sketch</strong> button in the top-right toolbar (or in the Sketch Palette) to return to the 3D Solid workspace.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3 — Drawing the 2D centerline wireframe with straight lines and tangent bend arcs</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-3-draw.png" alt="Step 3: Completed 2D centerline sketch in Fusion 360 with straight lines and tangent corner fillets" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Use the Structural Member Plugin to Populate 3D Tube Profiles</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              With your centerline sketch complete, use the <strong>Structural Member</strong> add-in to generate real hollow structural section (HSS) or round pipe solids along each sketch segment and trim their intersections:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Launch Structural Member Command (4a):</strong> Open <strong>CREATE &rarr; Structural Member &rarr; Structural Member Command</strong> (or click the <span class="ui-chip">SM</span> shortcut icon directly in the top ribbon). Notice that the same submenu and toolbar also provide <strong>Miter Command</strong>, <strong>Notch Command</strong>, and <strong>Trim Extend Command</strong> for coping and mitering joints between members.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Select Sketch Lines &amp; Choose Profile Dimensions (4b):</strong> Click a sketch segment in the viewport, then select your standard and cross-section in the <strong>STRUCTURAL MEMBER COMMAND</strong> dialog—for example, <strong>Select Standard:</strong> <code class="mono">ANSI_AISC</code>, <strong>Select Family:</strong> <code class="mono">HSS</code>, <strong>Select Size:</strong> <code class="mono">1-1/2 x 1-1/2</code> (<code class="mono">38.1 &times; 38.1 mm</code>), and <strong>Select Thickness:</strong> <code class="mono">1/8</code> (<code class="mono">3.17 mm</code>). Click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Populate Remaining Segments &amp; Notch/Trim Joints (4c):</strong> Repeat for the remaining sketch segments (here using round pipe along the U-shaped loop, creating components <code class="mono">SM0001:1</code> through <code class="mono">SM0007:1</code> in the Browser tree) and use <strong>Notch / Trim Extend</strong> so the round loop saddles cleanly against the vertical square post.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4a — Launching Structural Member Command (plus Miter, Notch &amp; Trim Extend) from the Create menu</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-4a-sm-menu.png" alt="Step 4a: Selecting Structural Member Command from the Fusion 360 Create menu" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4b — Configuring ANSI_AISC HSS 1-1/2 × 1-1/2 × 1/8 wall on the vertical post segment</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-4b-sm-dialog.png" alt="Step 4b: Configuring ANSI_AISC HSS 1-1/2 x 1-1/2 x 1/8 in the Structural Member Command dialog" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4c — All 7 structural member segments (SM0001:1 – SM0007:1) generated and trimmed</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-4c-sm-complete.png" alt="Step 4c: Completed 3D structural member assembly with SM0001:1 through SM0007:1 in the Fusion 360 Browser" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Combine Contiguous Tube Segments <em>(Required Only for Cut-and-Bend Support)</em></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              By default, the Structural Member plugin creates a separate component/body for every individual line and arc in your sketch (<code class="mono">SM0002:1</code> through <code class="mono">SM0007:1</code>).
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>If you are only cutting straight discrete tubes:</strong> Leave each structural member as its own separate body.</li>
              <li><strong>IF you want RotaryCAM Cut-and-Bend unfolding support:</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip">Modify &rarr; Combine</span> in the top toolbar, select the first segment of the bent tube as the <strong>Target Body</strong>, select all contiguous bend elbows and straight legs of that same tube as <strong>Tool Bodies</strong>, set <strong>Operation</strong> to <strong>Join</strong> (with <em>New Component</em> and <em>Keep Tools</em> unchecked), and click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout info">
              <span class="doc-callout-icon">💡</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Why Combine Is Essential for Bending:</strong> Merging the straight legs and tangent elbows of a bent tube into a <strong>single continuous solid body</strong> tells RotaryCAM&rsquo;s 3D unfolding solver that those segments are fabricated from one continuous stick of stock with kerf-bending relief cuts (see <a href="bending.html" style="color: var(--cyan-arc);">Tutorial 04: Bending HSS &amp; HSS-O</a>). Do <em>not</em> combine separate tubes that are welded together at joints (keep the vertical square post <code class="mono">SM0001:1</code> separate from the curved round loop).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5 — Using Modify → Combine (Join) to merge contiguous straight and bent segments into one solid body</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-5-combine.png" alt="Step 5: Combining contiguous straight and curved structural member segments into a single solid body in Fusion 360" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Export a STEP File (<code class="mono">.step</code> / <code class="mono">.stp</code>) of the Entire Design</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              You do not need to export parts one by one—RotaryCAM ingests multi-body STEP assemblies directly:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Open File &rarr; Export:</strong> Click the <strong>File</strong> menu icon in the top-left application bar and select <strong>Export...</strong></li>
              <li><strong>2. Select STEP Format:</strong> In the <strong>Export</strong> modal, set <strong>Type</strong> to <span class="ui-chip">STEP Files (*.stp *.step)</span>.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Export</span>:</strong> Save the <code class="mono">.step</code> file of your entire design to disk.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6 — Exporting the entire Fusion 360 assembly as a STEP file (*.stp *.step)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-6-export.png" alt="Step 6: Exporting the entire Fusion 360 design as a STEP file" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-7">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Load the Exported STEP File into RotaryCAM</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Switch to RotaryCAM (<code class="mono">Pipe CAM</code>) and ingest your exported <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo.step</code> file:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Drop or Select Your STEP File (7a):</strong> Drag and drop <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo.step</code> into the <strong>CAD INGESTION (Drop STEP or ZIP Bundle)</strong> box in the left sidebar (or click to browse your filesystem).</li>
              <li><strong>2. Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Load &amp; Process CAD</span> (7b):</strong> RotaryCAM immediately previews the full 3D assembly in the viewport. Click <strong>Load &amp; Process CAD</strong> (with <code class="mono">☑ One-Shot</code> enabled in the top bar to automatically run feature extraction, unfolding, and G-code generation).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7a — Dragging or selecting the exported .step file in the CAD Ingestion panel</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-7a-drop.png" alt="Step 7a: Dropping the exported STEP file into the RotaryCAM CAD Ingestion panel" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7b — Previewing rotarycam-demo.step in 3D and clicking Load &amp; Process CAD</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-7b-load.png" alt="Step 7b: 3D preview of rotarycam-demo.step and clicking Load & Process CAD" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 8
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-8">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 08</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Verify Flawless AI Cross-Section Profile Autodetection</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              As soon as CAD processing completes, RotaryCAM&rsquo;s neural network (<code class="mono">FOVNet</code>) classifies every solid in the assembly without any manual dimension entry:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Cross-Section &amp; Dimensions (<span class="ui-chip">AI</span> Badge in Left Sidebar):</strong> Inspect the <strong>CROSS-SECTION &amp; DIMENSIONS</strong> card on the left—RotaryCAM automatically identifies <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect</code>, outer dimensions <code class="mono">38.1&times;38.1 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1-1/2&Prime; &times; 1-1/2&Prime;</code>), wall thickness <code class="mono">3.17 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1/8&Prime;</code>), and stock length <code class="mono">110.0 mm</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Automatic Profile Grouping in Multi-Part Pack Nesting:</strong> Open the <strong>CUTTING PROFILE</strong> dropdown in the center workspace—both distinct cross-sections in the assembly (<code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (1 part)</code> and <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round (OD 42.2mm) (1 part)</code>) are automatically separated into their own stock-compatible nesting groups, ready for simulation and G-code emission.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 8 — Flawless AI profile autodetection of both Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1×38.1×3.17mm) and Profile_HSS_Round (OD 42.2mm)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/ai-cad-8-autodetect.png" alt="Step 8: RotaryCAM AI Cross-Section & Dimensions card and Multi-Part Pack Nesting dropdown showing autodetected rectangular and round tube profiles" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
